<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Man-Made River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">begun 1980s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Great Man-Made River Project</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Man-Made River is a large pipeline network in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, begun in the 1980s, that pumps fossil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">aquifers</a> beneath the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> to coastal cities. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a> draw a parallel between it and the ancient Garamantes' exploitation of the same non-renewable water.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-man-made-river.jpg" alt="Great Man-Made River" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Danmichaelo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Man_Made_River_schematic_EN.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1953 a search for oil in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> led to the discovery of large quantities of drinkable fossil water underground. The Great Man-Made River was conceived in the late 1960s. On 3 October 1983 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-peoples-congress">General People's Congress</a> held an extraordinary session that decided to fund and carry out the project, and on 28 August 1984 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muammar-gaddafi">Muammar Gaddafi</a> laid the foundation stone in the Sarir area. Construction was divided into five phases, and the first required 85 million cubic metres of excavation. The Libyan government paid for it, without loans from world banks, and the cost was projected at more than 25 billion US dollars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The project is a network of pipes that carries water from the Nubian Sandstone Aquifer System in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> to cities on the northern coast, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and Benghazi, over distances of up to 1,600 kilometres. According to the project's website it has 2,820 kilometres of pipes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> and more than 1,300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, most of them over 500 metres deep, and supplies 6,500,000 cubic metres of water a day to cities including Tripoli, Benghazi and Sirte. The first phase's water reached the Ajdabiya reservoir on 11 September 1989, and water reached Tripoli in 1996.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">aquifer</a> accumulated during the last ice age and is not being replenished. One estimate held that at the rates of 2007 the water could last a thousand years, while others suggested it could be exhausted in 60 to 100 years. Analysts say the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> system costs about a tenth as much as desalination.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The pipes were made at plants at Brega and Sarir, inaugurated on 28 August 1986, from pre-stressed concrete cylinders wound with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> wire largely made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. A South Korean firm, the Dong Ah Consortium, was the primary contractor for the first phases, and goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, mainly Italy, arrived by sea at Brega on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-sirte">Gulf of Sidra</a>. UNESCO has provided training to its engineers and technicians since 1990. Gaddafi described the project as the Eighth Wonder of the World. On 22 July 2011, during the First Libyan Civil War, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> air strike hit the Brega pipe plant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Man-Made River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Libya's Great Man-Made River Project</b>, begun in the 1980s, pumps from the same deep aquifers the Garamantes exploited, a modern case of the dependence on one resource that governed the civilisation's rise and fall.</blockquote><a href="/empire/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
